---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Grade from '../components/Grade.astro';
import State from '../components/State.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Timeline from '../components/Timeline.astro';
import { hms } from '../data/timelines';
---
<Base
  title="Harvard Medical School, George Church and Joseph Thakuria"
  description="What public records show about introduced-donor gifts to George Church, Epstein’s enrolment in the Personal Genome Project, the 2013 tissue-sample dispute and the June 2014 sequencing invoice."
  eyebrow="Harvard Medical School · Genetics"
  lede="The medical school is where the record comes closest to genetics. It shows gifts from introduced donors, enrolment as a research subject, a 2013 dispute over a tissue sample, and one small sequencing job. The larger proposals on the same invoice were never funded."
  reviewed="2026-11-20"
  basis="Harvard’s 2020 report; CNN and STAT reporting on Justice Department records"
>
  <h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
  <p>
    Professor George Church leads a genetics laboratory at Harvard Medical School and founded the Personal Genome
    Project, a study in which volunteers share their genomes and tissue for research. Epstein’s foundation supported
    his lab before the 2008 conviction, and Professor Church stayed in contact afterward. He apologised publicly for
    that in 2019.<Cite id="stat-2019-church" />
  </p>
  <p>
    Harvard’s 2020 report records that donors Epstein had introduced gave $2 million for Professor Church’s work
    between 2010 and 2015.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /> Documents released by the Justice Department in 2026 added
    three things: Epstein had been enrolled as a research subject, a 2013 attempt to prioritise his sample caused a
    dispute inside the lab, and a physician colleague sent him a priced list of genetic services in
    2014.<Cite id="stat-2026-tissue" /><Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" />
  </p>

  <h2 id="timeline">Timeline</h2>
  <Timeline events={hms} id="hms" label="Harvard Medical School timeline" />

  <h2 id="tissue">The 2013 tissue-sample dispute</h2>
  <p>
    STAT reported that in 2013 there was a push in the Church lab to move one person’s tissue sample ahead of samples
    that had waited years for sequencing. When lab members learned whose it was, they objected, and the objections
    stopped any special treatment. Professor Church confirmed the episode, said he had not personally pressed for
    priority, and said he “made the wrong call” on conflicting information.<Cite id="stat-2026-tissue" />
  </p>
  <p>
    A follow-up reported that cell lines containing Epstein’s DNA were created in 2013, that the present whereabouts of
    those cells are unknown, and that the consent date on the study profile believed to be his was altered on 31 January
    2026 by a person not identified.<Cite id="stat-2026-mystery" /> These points rest on one outlet’s reporting and are
    graded accordingly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="invoice">The June 2014 invoice</h2>
  <p>
    Dr Joseph Thakuria was then a senior physician at Massachusetts General Hospital and a colleague of Professor
    Church. A Harvard representative told CNN he was not directly employed by Harvard or its Wyss Institute. He said
    Professor Church introduced Epstein to him as a potential Personal Genome Project research subject.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" />
  </p>
  <p>
    The invoice he sent in June 2014 is one document with two very different parts. The table separates them.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap full">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>Line items on the June 2014 invoice, as reported from the Justice Department release</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th><th>Evidence state</th><th>What the record shows</th><th>Grade</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Exome sequencing from a saliva sample</th>
          <td data-label="Amount">$1,000</td>
          <td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="activity" /></td>
          <td data-label="What the record shows">Part of the initial $2,000. Dr Thakuria told CNN a $2,000 cheque covered DNA sequencing.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td>
          <td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Sequencing of fibroblasts already in storage</th>
          <td data-label="Amount">$1,000</td>
          <td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="activity" /></td>
          <td data-label="What the record shows">The second half of the initial $2,000.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td>
          <td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Stem-cell line creation</th>
          <td data-label="Amount">From $10,000</td>
          <td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="intent" /></td>
          <td data-label="What the record shows">Priced on the invoice. CNN found no record of payment.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td>
          <td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>“Personalized longevity studies” using gene editing</th>
          <td data-label="Amount">Priced</td>
          <td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="intent" /></td>
          <td data-label="What the record shows">A proposal to use gene editing on cells. No record that it was funded or performed.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td>
          <td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>“The Venus Project”</th>
          <td data-label="Amount">$160,000</td>
          <td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="intent" /></td>
          <td data-label="What the record shows">The largest single item. Dr Thakuria called it a preliminary idea of Epstein’s that “never went anywhere” and was never funded.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td>
          <td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>All proposed work together</th>
          <td data-label="Amount">$193,400</td>
          <td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="intent" /></td>
          <td data-label="What the record shows">The total had everything been commissioned. Only the first $2,000 is supported by any statement of payment.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td>
          <td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Callout title="How firm is the $2,000?">
    <p>
      The payment rests on Dr Thakuria’s own statement that a $2,000 cheque covered sequencing. CNN reported that it
      found no record of Epstein paying for the proposed services beyond that. We therefore mark the sequencing as
      documented activity and everything else on the invoice as stated intent. No source reviewed here describes the
      results of the sequencing or any use made of them.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Massachusetts General Hospital said it has no record of approving Dr Thakuria for the studies described in the
    emails, and that he left the hospital in 2022. Neither he nor Professor Church is accused of wrongdoing in the
    reporting.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" />
  </p>

  <h2 id="rating">The 2012 donor rating</h2>
  <p>
    In May 2026 the Crimson reported that Harvard Medical School’s development system generated a $75 million “active”
    donor rating for Epstein in July 2012, four years after Harvard had stopped accepting his gifts, and that the rating
    did not appear in the 2020 report.<Cite id="crimson-2026-hms-rating" /> A rating of this kind is an estimate of
    giving capacity. It does not show that a gift was sought or received. The report rests on a single outlet, and
    Harvard declined to comment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What the record does not show</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No gift from Epstein to Harvard Medical School after 2008.</li>
    <li>No evidence that the stem-cell, gene-editing or “Venus” proposals were carried out.</li>
    <li>No published results from the 2014 sequencing and no documented downstream use.</li>
    <li>No disciplinary finding against Professor Church or Dr Thakuria.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For how this fits with the other DNA-related material, see the <a href="/dna-genetics/">DNA and genetics record</a>.</p>
</Base>
